# 6. Recap: your practice portfolio

Choose one artefact you have already tried.

What does it help a person do?
What result would show it works?

---

# ALTs and assessed coursework

Four ALTs are collaborative learning contexts:
information systems, analytics, models and embedded systems.

Individual coursework follows the relevant SEC brief.
Practice folders and shared solutions are not finished submissions.

---

# Turn a goal into a requirement

“Make search useful” is hard to test.

“Show a no-match message when no record matches” is testable.

Name the input and expected output before running the test.

---

# Keep five small pieces of evidence

Start here: need and run instructions.
Design: sketch and two requirements.
Code/data: working files and source credits.
Tests: expected, actual, change and retest.
Reflection: contribution, limit and next step.

These are practice headings, not SEC submission rules.

---

# One useful improvement

Before: an absent-title search leaves old results.
Change: clear old results before displaying the response.
Retest: no-match message appears; a normal search still works.

Keep the earlier failure: it explains the change.

---

# Teamwork and explanation

Rotate developer, tester and other roles.
Driver enters code; navigator checks reasoning.

Explain input → process → output without reading every line.
Name what you contributed and what came from an example.

---

# Practice self-check

0–4 each: needs, implementation, testing,
communication, responsible choices. Total 20.

This is our classroom rubric, not an SEC scheme.
Choose one small next improvement.
